{
  "version": 1,
  "provenance": {
    "debug_labels": "US and JP Sio debug setup loops over five labels and selects two flag strings with boolean indexes. The EU native setup at 080C34C8 stores menu count ten and directly loads offsets 0 through 36 from the table at 09F7E728, verified at literal 080C3680. Its two additional stored CARD and ABILITY label pointers are separate dormant scalars, not fictitious boolean choices. The obsolete EU gUnk_09EF3504 alias pointed inside the ten-label table and has no active EU source use. All encoded labels are exact first-terminator Shift-JIS literals.",
    "mode": "The Mode immediately following each label run has the exact ASCII name mode_sio_dbg_flg and the native ordered mode_sio_dbg_flg_0/1/2 callback triple. Its common definition lives with those callbacks in mode_sio_dbg.c; native EU table growth changes only placement.",
    "source_boundary": "The first four code functions form SIO debug setup, update, teardown and battle-option preparation. The remaining mode_sio2.c functions own Pooh mode and animation records. Private debug RAM is two bytes with alignment one; private Pooh RAM begins at the next eight-byte boundary, with compiler-aligned TaskPool objects. Ordinary data and rodata sections preserve each mode record and name. The SIO-debug label pointers now share the native initialized-data section with their Mode record; the encoded text payloads retain their existing asset definitions.",
    "sio_debug_owner": "The US/JP setup callback stores a count of five and iterates those five label pointers, then reads the two flag labels through boolean indexes. EU stores ten and explicitly reads all ten active entries; its two additional four-byte label pointers remain separate dormant scalars. These regional arrays, the exact following Mode callback tuple, and the original lower-ROM readonly name consistently form one owner. Commit 8812570a introduced the top-level pointer const qualifiers together with recovery sections, providing no earlier source-level qualifier evidence. Mutable pointer arrays and scalar pointers, with const character pointees, naturally reproduce the complete 44-byte US/JP or 64-byte EU initialized-data run and unchanged 17-byte readonly name. This storage qualification is inferred from the original native organization, not from observed writes. All function bodies, original object extents/contents/relocations and private RAM are unchanged."
  },
